[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Closures: Capture Lists, Retain Cycles, and Escaping vs Non-Escaping in Swift"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Closures in Swift are self-contained blocks of functionality that can be passed around and used in your code. Understanding how they manage memory and execution flow is critical for writing robust iOS applications."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Escaping vs Non-Escaping Closures"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"By default, closures in Swift are non-escaping. This means the closure is called within the function it was passed to and executes before the function returns."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"func performAction(completion: () -> Void) {
    completion()
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"An escaping closure, marked with the @escaping keyword, outlives the function it was passed into. It is typically stored for asynchronous execution, such as network requests."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"class NetworkManager {
    func fetchData(completion: @escaping (Data) -> Void) {
        DispatchQueue.global().async {
            let dummyData = Data()
            completion(dummyData)
        }
    }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"heading","text":"Retain Cycles and Memory Management"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Closures are reference types. When a closure captures a variable or property from its surrounding context, it holds a strong reference to it. If the captured object also holds a strong reference to the closure, a retain cycle (memory leak) occurs."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"class ViewModel {
    var onDataFetched: (() -> Void)?

    func setupFetching() {
        NetworkManager().fetchData { [weak self] data in
            guard let self = self else { return }
            self.handleData(data)
        }
    }

    func handleData(_ data: Data) {
        print("Data handled")
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"heading","text":"Capture Lists"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Capture lists define how variables are captured inside a closure. They are placed at the beginning of the closure expression, enclosed in square brackets."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"[weak self]: Captures self as optional. If self is deallocated, it automatically becomes nil. Use this when self can become nil during execution."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"[unowned self]: Captures self as a non-optional reference. Use this only when you are certain self will never be nil while the closure is alive, otherwise accessing it will crash."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"[value = self.someProperty]: Allows capturing and renaming values or evaluating expressions at the moment the closure is created."},{"order":16,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mastering closures requires careful consideration of memory lifecycles. Always use @escaping intentionally, and protect your classes from retain cycles by utilizing weak or unowned capture lists."}]
